  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "The number $2\\frac{2}{3}$ has a rather amusing property:\n$$\\sqrt{2\\frac{2}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the positive integer $b$ for which\n$$\\sqrt{3\\frac{3}{b}}=3\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{b}}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$b=8$.",
          "worked": "The mixed-number radicand is $3+\\frac{3}{b}$. Squaring both sides gives\n$$\\begin{align*}3+\\frac{3}{b}&=9\\cdot\\frac{3}{b}\\\\3b+3&=27\\\\3b&=24\\\\b&=8.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth original sides are nonnegative, so the squaring is reversible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the positive values of $n$ for which\n$$\\sqrt{n\\frac{n}{15}}=n\\sqrt{\\frac{n}{15}}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=4$.",
          "worked": "The left radicand is $n+\\frac{n}{15}$. Squaring both sides gives\n$$\\begin{align*}n+\\frac{n}{15}&=n^2\\cdot\\frac{n}{15}\\\\16n&=n^3\\\\n^2&=16\\\\n&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by $n$ is valid because $n>0$. Both original sides are nonnegative, and $n=4$ satisfies the equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Suppose $n$, $a$ and $b$ are all positive integers. Find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$ in terms of $n$ if\n$$\\sqrt{n\\frac{a}{b}}=n\\sqrt{\\frac{a}{b}}.$$\nYour answer will show that there are infinitely many numbers with this property.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{n}{n^2-1}$, for integers $n\\ge2$.\n\nSquare both sides and rearrange. Each $n\\ge2$ gives a distinct example.",
          "worked": "The left radicand is $n+\\frac{a}{b}$. Squaring both sides gives\n$$\\begin{align*}n+\\frac{a}{b}&=n^2\\frac{a}{b}\\\\n&=(n^2-1)\\frac{a}{b}\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=\\frac{n}{n^2-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThere is no solution when $n=1$. For each integer $n\\ge2$, choose $a=n$ and $b=n^2-1$. Both sides are nonnegative, so these choices satisfy the original equation. The resulting mixed numbers lie strictly between $n$ and $n+1$, giving infinitely many distinct examples.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
  },
